However, before the btrfs_cow_block() call inside the cow block, there is no explicit lock-unlock sequence for "b" just before that call, except that a check for correct write_lock_level is performed. But...
- If write_lock_level is already >= current level, code proceeds.
- The assignment p->locks[level] = root_lock happens only on the root node logic; for other nodes, the logic is in the "if (!p->skip_locking)" block.

There is room for inconsistency if the locks are not taken strictly as needed, i.e., if logic fails to upgrade to write lock before COW, a race condition (the CVE) can happen.

